<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Cameroon</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Yaoundé</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Central African republic formed from French and British mandates in 1960 and 1961, led by Paul Biya since 1982, with an Anglophone separatist conflict.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-cameroon">French Cameroon</a>, a French mandate and then a UN trust territory, became independent on 1 January 1960 as the Republic of Cameroon under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadou-ahidjo">Ahmadou Ahidjo</a>. On 1 October 1961 the southern part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-cameroons">British Cameroons</a> joined it, forming the Federal Republic of Cameroon of East and West Cameroon.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ahidjo's Cameroon National Union became the sole legal party on 1 September 1966, and a referendum on 20 May 1972 replaced the federation with a United Republic. The UPC, which had campaigned for independence, fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war until 1971. Ahidjo resigned on 4 November 1982, and Paul Biya succeeded him and restored the name Republic of Cameroon by decree in 1984. Multi-party politics returned in December 1990. In 2008 Cameroon took control of the Bakassi peninsula from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Since November 2016 protests in the English-speaking Northwest and Southwest regions have grown into an armed separatist conflict, and more than 450,000 people have fled their homes since 2016. Cameroon declared victory over Boko Haram on its territory in September 2018. Biya remains president, and protests followed the 2025 election after allegations of fraud by the opposition.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cameroon.jpg" alt="Cameroon" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: (of code) -xfi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cameroon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yaoundé</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ahmadou Ahidjo, Paul Biya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Islam, traditional faiths</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cameroon">Cameroon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Cameroon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=cameroon" aria-label="Cameroon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5000 2033 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cameroon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5320 2289L5320 2289M5320 2289L5256 2278"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5320 2289h0M5256 2278h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5320 2289h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5320 2289h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yaoundé. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=cameroon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
